## 15. Privacy Analysis

| Technique            | Privacy Protection                                          | Data Utility | Main Limitation                           |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| Data Masking         | Hides parts of identifiers                                  | High         | May still permit identification           |
| Removing Identifiers | Removes direct identifiers                                  | High         | Other attributes may identify individuals |
| Generalization       | Reduces identifying detail                                  | Medium/High  | Some information is lost                  |
| K-Anonymity          | Groups individuals based on quasi-identifiers               | Medium       | Vulnerable to some inference attacks      |
| Differential Privacy | Limits information about individuals in statistical outputs | Medium/High  | Noise can reduce accuracy                 |

## 16. Privacy-Utility Trade-Off

An important concept in anonymization is the **privacy-utility trade-off**.

Increasing privacy protection can reduce the usefulness or accuracy of the data.

For example:

```text
More Generalization
        ↓
Less Detailed Data
        ↓
Higher Privacy
        ↓
Lower Data Utility
```

On the other hand, keeping highly detailed information can make the dataset more useful for analysis but may increase privacy risks.

Therefore, an organization should select an anonymization method according to the intended use of the data and the associated privacy risks.

